---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `let scores = [88, 92, 79, 95];

for (let i = 0; i <= scores.length; i++) {
  console.log(scores[i]);
}`;
const Bug2 = `let names = ["Avery", "Blake", "Casey"];

console.log("Last:", names[names.length]);`;
const Bug3 = `let original = [10, 20, 30];
let backup = original;

original[0] = 999;

console.log("Working:", original);
console.log("Backup: ", backup);`;
const Bug4 = `let values = [4, 8, 15, 16];
let total = 0;

console.log("Adding values...");

for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
  let total = total + values[i];
}

console.log("Total:", total);`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Arrays</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>Four short programs about lists. Two of them involve an index that is off by one &mdash; and the two behave completely differently, which is the point.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Three kinds of mistake show up in JavaScript.</b> A
            <b class="text-sn-red">syntax error</b> stops the program before it
            runs. A <b class="text-sn-orange">reference error</b> runs until it
            hits the bad name, so you get <i>partial output and then a
            crash</i> &mdash; that partial output is how you tell it from a
            syntax error. A <b class="text-sn-yellow">logic error</b> runs
            cleanly to the end and gives the wrong answer, which is the one
            that will cost you marks on an exam.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Three of these 4 are logic errors.</b> If you find
            yourself waiting for an error message on those, that is exactly the
            habit this challenge exists to break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>This should print every score. It prints <code>undefined</code> at the end.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 1"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Also:</b> The same off-by-one written in exam notation would not print <code>undefined</code>. Say what it would do instead.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>This should print the last name in the list. It prints nothing useful.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 2"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>This should make a backup, change the working copy, and leave the backup alone. Both end up changed.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 3"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Also:</b> This one has no fix using only what Activity 1.23 taught. Say in one sentence what is actually happening, and what the exam-notation version would have done differently.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>This should add up every value. It prints its heading and then crashes.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 4"
            class="mb-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each program, give:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Which kind of mistake it is &mdash; syntax, reference, or logic</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Work them out by reading first. Running the code tells you
            <i>that</i> something is wrong; it rarely tells you <i>why</i>, and
            on the exam there is no computer.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
